%I A401382 #20 Nov 04 2026 12:34:10
%S A401382 2,4,9,12,14,16,18,24,25,48,54,60,62,64,78,90,96,112,114,124,126,132,
%T A401382 140,150,168,174,182,192,198,204,222,234,240,246,252,254,258,264,276,
%U A401382 289,294,306,308,336,354,360,366,372,378,390,402,408,414,434,438,448,474,480,498,508,528,532,534,540,552,558,564
%N A401382 Numbers k such that {the largest divisor of sigma(k) coprime with k} is a prime, where sigma is the sum of divisors function.
%C A401382 Odd terms of this sequence seem to be given by A193065, which is true if Pomerance's conjecture concerning A105402 is true. Note that if there existed an odd p-perfect number which were a multiple of odd prime p, then it would be included in A193065, but not in this sequence. See also comments in A401388. - _Antti Karttunen_, Nov 02 2026
%H A401382 Antti Karttunen, <a href="/A401382/b401382.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401382 <a href="/index/O#opnseqs">Index entries for sequences where odd perfect numbers must occur, if they exist at all</a>.
%F A401382 {k such that A010051(A401375(k)) = 1}.
%o A401382 (PARI) is_A401382(n) = { my(s=sigma(n)); fordiv(s,d,if(1==gcd(s/d,n), return(isprime(s/d)))); };
%Y A401382 Cf. A000203, A010051, A105402, A193065 (conjectured to give the odd terms), A401375, A401388, A401389.
%Y A401382 Subsequence of A401381.
%K A401382 nonn,new
%O A401382 1,1
%A A401382 _Antti Karttunen_, Oct 31 2026
